== 4. The Saints: Division

#strong[#emph[Thirdly,] Satan has his devices to destroy the saints, and one great device that he has to destroy the saints is,]

BY working them first to be estranged,#footnote[Gal 5:15. If we knock, we break; dissolution is the daughter of dissension.] then to divide, then to be bitter and suspicious, and then to be “biting and devouring one another.” Our own woeful experience is too great a proof of this. The Israelites in Egypt did not vex one another more than Christians in these days have done, which has brought a deadly consumption on some.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy against this device of Satan is to dwell more on one another’s graces than on one another’s weaknesses and infirmities. It is sad to consider that saints should have#footnote[Flavius Vespasianus (the emperor) was more ready to conceal the vices of his friends than their virtues. Can you#super[+] think seriously of this, Christians (that a heathen should excel you#super[+]), and not blush? 2 Cor 2:7, 8.] many eyes to see one another’s infirmities, and not one eye to see each other’s graces; that they should use spectacles to look at one another’s weaknesses, rather than mirrors to look at one another’s graces. Erasmus tells of one who collected all the lame and defective verses in Homer’s works, but passed over all that were excellent. Ah, if only this were not the practice of many who will at last meet in heaven! If only they were not so careful and skillful to collect all the weaknesses of others, and to pass over all those things that are excellent in them! The Corinthians looked more at the incestuous man’s sin than at his sorrow, which was likely to have drowned him in sorrow.

Tell me, saints, is it not a sweeter, more comforting and delightful thing to look more on one another’s graces than on one another’s infirmities? Tell me, what pleasure, what delight, what comfort is there in looking on the enemies, the wounds, the sores, the sicknesses, the diseases, the nakedness of our friends? Now sin, you#super[+] know, is the soul’s enemy, the soul’s wound, the soul’s sores, the soul’s sickness, the soul’s#footnote[#emph[Non Gens, sed mens, non Genus sed Genius:] not race, or place, but grace, truly sets forth a man.] disease, the soul’s nakedness. And ah! What a heart that man has who loves to look on these! Grace is the choicest flower in all a Christian’s garden; it is the richest jewel in all his crown; it is his princely robes; it is the height of royalty; and therefore it must be the most pleasing, sweet and delightful object for a gracious eye to be fixed on. Sin is darkness, grace is light; sin is hell, grace is heaven. And what madness it is to look more at darkness than at light, more at hell than at heaven!#footnote[Jas 5:11; 2:25. 1 Pet 2:6 #emph[vide] \[see\]. Sin is Satan’s work, grace is God’s work; and is it not most fitting that the child should look most at, and mind most, his Father’s work, etc.?]

Tell me, saints, does not God look more on his people’s graces than on their weaknesses? Surely he does. He looks more at David’s and Asa’s uprightness than at their infirmities, though they were great and many. He looks more at Job’s patience than at his outbursts: “Remember the patience of Job”—not a word of his impatience, etc. He who painted Alexander while he had a scar on his face painted him with his finger on the scar. God puts his finger on his people’s scars, so that no blemish may appear. Ah saints! If only you#super[+] would make it the height of your#super[+] glory in this to be like your#super[+] heavenly Father! By so doing much sin would be prevented, the designs of wicked men frustrated, Satan outwitted, many wounds healed, many sad hearts cheered, and God more abundantly honored, etc.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that love and unity make most for your#super[+] own safety and security. We will#footnote[There was a Temple of Concord among the heathen; and will concord not be found among Christians, who are temples of the Holy Spirit?] be #emph[insuperabiles] if we are #emph[inseparabiles]: invincible, if we are inseparable. The world may frown on you#super[+] and plot against you#super[+], but it cannot hurt you#super[+]. Unity is the best bond of safety in every church and commonwealth. And the Scythian king in Plutarch represented this vividly to his eighty sons. When he was ready to die, he ordered a bundle of arrows, bound fast together, to be given to his sons to break. They all tried to break them, but because they were bound fast together, they could not. Then he had the band cut, and then they#footnote[Pancirollus says that the most precious pearl among the Romans was called #emph[unio,] union.] broke them with ease. He applied it like this: “My sons, as long as you#super[+] keep together, you#super[+] will be invincible; but if the band of union between you#super[+] is broken, you#super[+] will easily be broken in pieces.” Pliny writes of a stone in the island of Scyrus that, if it is whole, though a large and heavy one, it floats on the water, but when it is broken, it sinks. So as long as saints keep whole, nothing will sink them; but if they break, they are in danger of sinking and drowning, etc.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to dwell on those commands of God that require you#super[+]#footnote[To act or run contrary to God’s express command, even under pretense of a revelation from God, is as much as a man’s life is worth, as you#super[+] may see in that sad story, 1 Kgs 13:24.] to love one another. Oh, when your#super[+] hearts begin to rise against each other, charge the commands of God on your#super[+] hearts, and say to your#super[+] souls, “O our souls! Has not the eternal God commanded you#super[+] to love those who love the Lord? And is it not life to obey, and death to rebel?” Therefore see that you#super[+] fulfill the commands of the Lord, for his commands are not like those that are easily reversed, but like those of the Medes, which cannot be changed. Oh, ponder much on these commands of God:#footnote[John 13:34.] “A new commandment I give you#super[+]: Love one another. As I have loved you#super[+], so you#super[+] also must love one another.” It is called a new#footnote[Some think it a Hebraism; in that language, new, rare and most excellent are synonyms. John 15:12, 17.] commandment because it is renewed in the gospel and brought home by Christ’s example, and because it is rare, choice, special and remarkable above all others. “This is My commandment, that you#super[+] love one another as I have loved you#super[+].” “This is My command to you#super[+]: Love one another.” “Be indebted to no one, except to one another in love. For he who loves his neighbor has#footnote[Rom 13:8.] fulfilled the law.” “Continue in brotherly love.”#footnote[Heb 13:1.] “Let us love one another, because love comes from#footnote[1 John 4:7.] God. Everyone who loves has been born of God and knows God.” “Love one another#footnote[1 Pet 1:22.] deeply, from a pure heart.” “Finally,#footnote[1 Pet 3:8.] all of you#super[+], be like-minded and sympathetic, love as brothers, be tenderhearted and humble.” “This is the message#footnote[1 John 3:11.] you#super[+] have heard from the beginning: We should love one another.” “And this is His#footnote[Verse 23.] commandment: that we should believe in the name of His Son, Jesus Christ, and we should love one another just as He commanded us.” “Beloved, if God so loved us, we also ought to love#footnote[1 John 4:11.] one another.”

Oh, dwell much on these precious commands, so that your#super[+] love may be#footnote[The ancients used to say commonly that Alexander and Hephaestion had but one soul in two distinct bodies, because their joy and sorrow, glory and disgrace were shared by them both.] inflamed toward one another. In the early times of the church it was much noticed by the heathen that in the depth of misery, when fathers and mothers forsook their children, Christians (otherwise strangers) stuck to one another; their love of religion proved firmer than that of nature. Ah, if only there were more of that spirit among the saints in these days! The world was once destroyed with water for the heat of lusts, and it is thought that it will be destroyed again with fire for the coldness of love.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell more on those choice and sweet things in which you#super[+] agree than on those things in which you#super[+] differ. Ah! If only you#super[+] did this, how sinful heats would be cooled, and your#super[+] love raised, and your#super[+] spirits sweetened toward one another! You#super[+] agree in most things; you#super[+] differ in only a few. You#super[+] agree in the greatest and weightiest, as concerning God, Christ, the Spirit, the Scripture, etc. You#super[+] differ only in those points that have long been disputed among men of the greatest piety and gifts. You#super[+] agree to acknowledge the Scripture, to hold to Christ the head, and to walk#footnote[What a sad thing it was that a heathen should say, “No beasts are so harmful to men as Christians are to one another.”] according to the laws of the new creation. Do Herod and Pilate agree? Do Turks and pagans agree? Do #emph[bears] and #emph[lions, tigers] and #emph[wolves,] indeed, does a legion of #emph[devils] agree in one body? And will not #emph[saints] agree, who differ only in such things as have the least of the heart of God in them, and that will never hinder your#super[+] meeting in heaven, etc.?

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that God delights to be called #emph[(Deus pacis) the God of peace;] and Christ to be called#footnote[2 Cor 13:11. Isa 9:6. #emph[Ubi pax ibi Christus, quia Christus pax:] where peace is, there is Christ, because Christ is peace.] #emph[(Princeps pacis) the Prince of Peace, and King of Salem,] that is, King of peace; and the Spirit is a Spirit of peace. “The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace,” Gal 5:22. Oh! Why then should not the saints be children of peace? Certainly men of perverse, unquiet, fiery spirits cannot have such sweet evidence of their share in the God of peace, in the Prince of Peace and in the Spirit of peace as those precious souls have who follow after the things that make for love and peace. The very name of peace#footnote[#emph[Dulce nomen pacis] \[Sweet is the name of peace\], said the orator. The Greeks had the statue of Peace with Pluto, the god of riches, in her arms.] is sweet and comforting; its fruit and effect are pleasant and profitable, more to be desired than countless triumphs. It is a blessing that ushers in a multitude of other blessings. The ancients used to paint Peace in the form of a woman with a horn of plenty in her hand. Ah! Peace and love among the saints is what will keep them and their mercies secure at home. Indeed, it will multiply their mercies; it will move the God of mercy to crown them with the choicest mercies; and it is what will make them most fearsome, invincible and successful out in the world. Love and peace among the saints is what brings the counsels of their enemies to a standstill and makes all their enterprises miscarry. It is what most weakens their hands, wounds their hopes and kills their hearts, etc.

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to make it more a matter of care and conscience to keep up your#super[+] peace#footnote[There is no fear of knowing too much, but there is much fear of practicing too little.] with God. Ah Christians! I am afraid that your#super[+] remissness here is what has caused much of the sourness, bitterness and divisions that are among you#super[+]. Ah! You#super[+] have not kept up your#super[+] peace with God as you#super[+] should, and that is why you#super[+] so dreadfully break the peace among yourselves#super[+]. The Lord has promised:#footnote[Prov 16:7.] “When a man’s ways please the #smallcaps[Lord], He makes even the man’s enemies live at peace with him.” Ah!#footnote[Pharnaces sent a crown to Caesar at the same time that he rebelled against him; but Caesar returned the crown, and this message with it (#emph[faceret imperata prius]): let him return to his obedience first. There is no sound peace to be had with God or man except in the way of obedience.] How much more, then, would God make the children of peace keep the peace among themselves, if only their ways pleased him? All created things are at his command and under his check. Laban followed Jacob with one troop and Esau met him with another, both with hostile intentions; but because Jacob’s ways pleased the Lord, God so works by his mighty power that Laban leaves him with a kiss, and Esau meets him with a kiss. He has an oath from the one, tears from the other, and peace with both. If we make it our business to keep up our league with God, God will make it his work and his glory to maintain our peace with men. But if men make light of keeping up their peace with God, it is just of God to leave them to a spirit of pride, envy, anger, contention, division and confusion; to leave them “biting and devouring one another” until they are “consumed by one another,” etc.

The seventh #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell much on that near relation and union that is between you#super[+]. This consideration had a sweet influence on Abraham’s heart: “So Abram said to Lot, ‘Please let there be#footnote[Gen 13:8. מריבה: Oh, let there be no bitterness between us, for we are brethren.] no contention between you and me, or between your herdsmen and my herdsmen. After all, we are brothers.’” That is a sweet word of the psalmist: “Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers live together in harmony!” It is not good without being pleasant,#footnote[The many-colored coats were the badges of the king’s children; so is following after peace now.] or pleasant without being good, but good and pleasant. There are some things that are (#emph[bona sed non jucunda]) good but not pleasant, as patience and discipline. There are some things that are pleasant but not good, as carnal pleasures, sensuality, etc. There are some things that are neither good nor pleasant, as malice, envy, worldly sorrow, etc. And there are some things that are both good and pleasant, as piety, love, peace and union among brethren. And oh, that we could see more of this among those who will one day meet (in their Father’s kingdom) and never part, etc.! And as they are brethren, so they are all fellow members:#footnote[1 Cor 12:27.] “Now you#super[+] are the body of Christ, and each of you#super[+] is a member of it.”

And again: “We are members of His body, of His flesh and of His bones.” Are#footnote[Eph 5:30.] the members of the natural body serviceable and useful to one another, and will the members of this spiritual body cut and destroy one another? Is it against the law of nature for the natural members to cut and slash one another? And is it not much more against the law of nature, and of grace, for the members of Christ’s glorious body to do so? And as you#super[+] are all fellow members, so you#super[+] are all fellow soldiers#footnote[Rev 12:7, 8. Heb 2:10.] under the same captain of salvation, the Lord Jesus, fighting against the world, the flesh and the devil. And as you#super[+] are all fellow soldiers, so you#super[+] are all fellow sufferers under the same enemies, the devil and the#footnote[Rev 2:10.] world. And as you#super[+] are all fellow sufferers,#footnote[John 15:19, 20. Heb 13:14. Heb 11:13.] so you#super[+] are fellow travelers toward the land of Canaan, #emph[the new Jerusalem that is above:] “Here we do not have a permanent city, but we are looking for the city that is to come.” The heirs of heaven are strangers on earth. And as you#super[+] are all fellow travelers, so you#super[+] are all fellow heirs of#footnote[Rom 8:17.] the same crown and inheritance.

The eighth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to dwell on the miseries of discord. Dissolution is the#footnote[Our dissensions are one of the Jews’ greatest stumbling blocks. Can you#super[+] think of it, and your#super[+] hearts not bleed?] daughter of dissension. Ah! How the name of Christ and the way of Christ suffer by the discord of saints! How many who are entering on the ways of God are hindered and saddened, and the mouths of the wicked opened and their hearts hardened against God and his ways, by the discord of his people! Remember this: the disagreement of Christians is the devil’s triumph. And what a sad thing this is, that Christians should give Satan cause to triumph! It was a notable saying of one: “Take away strife and call back peace, lest you lose a man, your friend, and the devil, an enemy, rejoice over you#super[+] both,” etc.

The ninth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that it is no disgrace to you#super[+] to be first in seeking peace and reconciliation, but rather an honor to you#super[+],#footnote[Those who first begin to pursue peace will have both the name and the renown, the comfort and the credit, of being most like God.] that you#super[+] have begun to seek peace. Abraham was the elder, and worthier than Lot, both in respect of grace and of nature also (for he was Lot’s uncle), and yet he first seeks peace with his inferior, which God has recorded to his honor. Ah! How the God of peace by his Spirit and messengers pursues peace with poor mortals! God first makes offers of peace to us:#footnote[2 Cor 5:20.] “Therefore we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making His appeal through us. We implore you#super[+] on behalf of Christ: Be reconciled to God.” God’s grace first kneels to us; and who can turn their backs on such blessed and bleeding embraces, except souls #emph[in whom Satan, the god of this world,] reigns as king? God is the party wronged, and yet he sues for peace with us first:#footnote[Isa 65:1.] “To a nation that did not call My name, I said, ‘Here I am! Here I am!’” Ah! How#footnote[“Here I am! Here I am!”: it is doubled to show God’s exceeding readiness to show favor and mercy to them.] the sweetness, the freeness and the riches of his grace break forth and shine on poor souls! When a man goes away from the sun, the sunbeams still follow him; so when we go away from the sun of righteousness, even then the beams of his love and mercy follow us. Christ first sent to Peter, who had denied him, and to the rest, who had forsaken him: “Go, tell His disciples and #emph[Peter], ‘He is going ahead of you#super[+] into Galilee.#footnote[Mark 16:7.] There you#super[+] will see Him, just as He told you#super[+].’”

Ah souls! It is not a base, low thing, but a godlike thing, though we are wronged by others, still to be the first in seeking peace. Such actions will show that much of God is with a man’s spirit, etc.

Christians! It is not a matter of liberty whether you#super[+] will or will not pursue peace; it is a matter of duty that lies on you#super[+]. You#super[+] are bound by express precepts to follow after peace; and though it may seem to flee from you#super[+], still you#super[+] must pursue it: “Pursue#footnote[Heb 12:14. Διώκετε: it means to follow after peace as the persecutor follows the one he persecutes. Ps 34:14.] peace with everyone, as well as holiness, without which no one will see the Lord.” Peace and holiness are to be pursued with the greatest eagerness that can be imagined. So the psalmist: “Turn away from evil and do good; #emph[seek] peace and #emph[pursue] it.” The Hebrew word that is rendered #emph[seek] here is in #emph[Piel,] and it means to seek earnestly, vehemently, ardently,#footnote[בקש] studiously, industriously. And #emph[pursue] it: that Hebrew word means earnestly#footnote[ורדפהו] to pursue, being a metaphor taken from the eagerness of wild beasts, or ravenous#footnote[a רדף] birds, which will run or fly both fast and far rather than be deprived of their prey. So the apostle presses the same duty on the Romans: “Let us pursue what leads to#footnote[Rom 14:19.] peace and to mutual edification.”

Ah! You#super[+] perverse, sour, surly Christians, can you#super[+] look on these commands of God without tears and blushing? I have read a remarkable story of Aristippus, though only a heathen,#footnote[Plutarch.] who went of his own accord to Aeschines, his enemy, and said, “Are we never to be reconciled until we become table talk to all the country?” And when Aeschines answered that he would most gladly be at peace with him, Aristippus said, “Remember then that though I was the elder and the better man, yet I sought you first.” “You are indeed,” said Aeschines, “a far better man than I; for I began the quarrel, but you the reconciliation.” My prayer is that this heathen may not rise in judgment against the flourishing professing Christians of our times,#footnote[Ps 64:3.] “who sharpen their tongues like swords and aim their bitter words like arrows.”

The tenth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is for saints to join together and walk together in the ways of grace and holiness as far as they do agree, making the word the only touchstone and judge of their actions. That is sweet advice that the apostle gives:#footnote[Phil 3:14.] “I press on toward the goal to win the prize of God’s heavenly calling in Christ Jesus. All of us#footnote[Verse 15.] who are perfect” (comparatively, or in their own estimation) “should embrace this point of view. And if you#super[+] think differently about some issue, God will reveal this to you#super[+] as well. Nevertheless, as far as#footnote[Verse 16.] we have already attained, let us walk by the same rule, let us be of the same mind.”

Ah Christians! God loses much, and you#super[+] lose much, and Satan gains much, because you#super[+] do not, and will not, walk lovingly together as far as your#super[+] ways lie together. It is your#super[+] sin and shame that you#super[+] do not, and will not, pray together, hear together, confer together, mourn together, etc., because#footnote[Great is the power of joint prayer. Mary Queen of Scots said that she feared the prayers of Mr. Knox and his companions more than an army of thirty thousand men.] in some far lesser things you#super[+] are not agreed. What folly and madness it is in those whose way of a hundred miles lies ninety-nine together, and yet who will not walk so far together because they cannot go the other mile together! Yet such is the folly and madness of many Christians in these days, who will not do many things they may do because they cannot do everything they should do. I fear God will whip them into a better frame before he is done with them. #emph[He will break their bones and pierce their hearts;] but he will cure them of this malady, etc.

And be sure you#super[+] make the word the only touchstone and judge of all persons and actions:#footnote[Isa 8:20.] “To the law and to the testimony! If they do not speak according to this word, they have no light of dawn.” It is best and safest to make that the judge of all men and things now by which all will be judged on the last day: “The word” (says Christ)#footnote[John 12:48.] “that I have spoken will judge him on the last day.” Do not make your#super[+] dim light, your#super[+] notions, your#super[+] fancies, your#super[+] opinions, the judge of men’s actions, but always judge by the rule, and plead, “It is written.” When a vain, insistent soul in a dispute with a holy man cried out, “Hear me, hear me!” the holy man answered, “Let neither you hear me, nor I you, but#footnote[Nec ego te, nec tu me, sed ambo audiamus Apostolum.] let us both hear the apostle.”

Constantine, in all the disputes before him with the Arians, would always call for the word of God, as the only way, if not to convert them, yet to stop their mouths, etc.

The eleventh #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to be much in self-judging:#footnote[1 Cor 11:31.] “Judge yourselves#super[+], and you#super[+] will not be judged by the Lord.” Ah! If Christians’#footnote[It is recorded of Nero that, being unclean himself, he thought no man was chaste.] hearts were more taken up with judging themselves and condemning themselves, they would not be so apt to judge and censure others, and to behave sourly and bitterly toward others who differ from them. There are no souls in the world so afraid to judge others as#footnote[In the Olympic games the wrestlers did not put their crowns on their own heads, but on the heads of others; so it is with souls who are good at self-judging.] those who most judge themselves, nor so careful to make a righteous judgment of men or things as those who are most careful to judge themselves. There are none in the world who so tremble to think evil of others, to speak evil of others, or to do evil to others, as those who make it their business to judge themselves. There are none who put such sweet constructions and charitable interpretations on men and things as those who are best at, and most in, judging themselves. One request I have to you#super[+] who are much in judging others and little in judging yourselves#super[+], to you#super[+] who are so apt and prone to judge rashly, falsely and unrighteously: that you#super[+] will every morning dwell a little on these Scriptures.

“Do not judge, or you#super[+] will be judged. For#footnote[Matt 7:1.] with the same judgment you#super[+] pronounce, you#super[+] will be judged; and with the measure you#super[+] use, it#footnote[Verse 2.] will be measured to you#super[+].” “Stop judging#footnote[John 7:24.] by outward appearances, and start judging justly.” “The one who does not eat everything must not judge#footnote[Rom 14:3.] the one who does, for God has accepted him.” “Why, then, do you judge your brother? Or why do you#footnote[Verse 10.] belittle your brother? For we will all stand before God’s judgment seat.” “Therefore let us stop judging one another. Instead,#footnote[Verse 13.] judge this: not to put any stumbling block or obstacle in your brother’s way.” “Judge nothing before the appointed time;#footnote[1 Cor 4:5.] wait until the Lord comes. He will bring to light what is hidden in darkness and will expose the motives of men’s hearts. At that time each will receive his praise from God.” “Do not slander one another” (brethren).#footnote[Jas 4:11.] “Anyone who speaks against his brother or judges him speaks against the law and judges it. And if you judge the law, you are not a practitioner of the law, but a judge of it. There is only one Lawgiver and Judge,#footnote[Verse 12.] the One who is able to save and destroy. But who are you to judge your neighbor?” “Who are you to judge#footnote[Rom 14:4.] someone else’s servant? To his own master he stands or falls. And he will stand, for the Lord is able to make him stand.”

One Delphidius was accusing another before Julian of something he could not prove. When the accused denied the fact, Delphidius answered, “If it is enough to deny what is laid to one’s charge, who will be found guilty?” Julian answered, “And if it is enough to be accused, who can be innocent?” You#super[+] are wise, and know how to apply it.

The twelfth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is this: above all, #emph[labor to be]#footnote[1 Pet 5:5.] “clothed with humility.” Humility makes a man peaceable among brethren, fruitful in well-doing, cheerful in suffering and constant in holy walking. Humility fits a man for the highest services we owe to Christ, and yet it will not neglect#footnote[John 13:5.] the lowest service to the lowliest saint. Humility can feed on the plainest dish, and yet it is maintained by the choicest delicacies, such as God, Christ and glory. Humility will make a man bless the one who curses him, and pray for those who persecute him. A humble heart is a dwelling for God, a scholar#footnote[Humility is (#emph[conservatrix virtutum]), says Bernard, that which keeps all graces together.] for Christ, a companion for angels, a preserver of grace and a preparer for glory. Humility is the nurse of our graces, the preserver of our blessings and the great promoter of holy duties. Humility cannot find three things on this side of heaven: it cannot find #emph[fullness in created things,] nor #emph[sweetness in sin,] nor #emph[life in an ordinance without Christ.] A humble soul always finds three things on this side of heaven: #emph[the soul to be empty, Christ to be full, and every mercy and duty to be sweet in which God is enjoyed.] Humility can weep over other men’s weaknesses, and joy and rejoice over their graces. Humility will make a man#footnote[1 Thess 1:2, 3.] quiet and content in the lowliest condition, and it will keep a man from envying others’ prosperity. Humility honors those who are strong in grace, and puts two hands#footnote[Eph 3:8.] under those who are weak in grace. Humility makes a man richer than other men, and it makes a man judge himself the poorest among men. Humility will see much good in others when it can see but little in itself. Ah Christians! Though faith is the champion of grace, and love the nurse of grace, yet humility is the beautifier of grace; it casts a general glory over all the graces in the soul. Ah! If Christians abounded more in humility,#footnote[The humble soul is like the violet, which grows low, hangs its head downward and hides itself with its own leaves; and were it not that the fragrant smell of its many virtues reveals it to the world, it would choose to live and die in its self-contenting secrecy.] they would be less bitter, perverse and sour, and they would be more gentle, meek and sweet in their spirits and practices. Humility will make a man have high thoughts of others and low thoughts of himself. It will make a man see much glory and excellence in others, and much baseness and sinfulness in himself; it will make a man see others rich and himself poor, others strong and himself weak, others wise and himself foolish. Humility will make a man excellent at covering others’ infirmities, at recording their gracious services and at delighting in their graces. It makes a man rejoice in every light that outshines his own, and in every wind that blows others good. Humility is better at believing than at questioning other men’s happiness. “I judge,” says a humble soul, “that it is well with these Christians now, but it will be far better with them hereafter. They are now on the borders of the new Jerusalem, and it will be but as a day before they slide into Jerusalem.” A humble soul is more willing to say, “Heaven is that man’s,” than “It is mine”; “Christ is that Christian’s,” than “He is mine”; and “God is their God in covenant,” than “He is mine.” Ah! If Christians were more humble, there would be less fire and more love among them than there is now, etc.
